<h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Choose the correct option for each of the following questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> The inertia of a body depends directly on its:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Velocity</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Mass</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Acceleration</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Shape</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> The S.I. unit of linear momentum is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) kg m s<sup>-2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) kg m s<sup>-1</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) N s<sup>-1</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) J s</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Newton's first law of motion provides the qualitative definition of:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Work</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Force</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Energy</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Power</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> A net force of 10 N acts on a body of mass 2 kg. The acceleration produced in the body is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 5 m s<sup>-2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 20 m s<sup>-2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 0.2 m s<sup>-2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 12 m s<sup>-2</sup></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> When a carpet is beaten with a stick, dust particles fall off due to:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Inertia of motion</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Inertia of rest</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Inertia of direction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Force of gravity only</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q6.</strong> The gravitational unit of force in the MKS system is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) dyne</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) kgf</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) gf</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Newton</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q7.</strong> The relationship between 1 Newton and 1 dyne is given by:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 1 N = 10<sup>3</sup> dynes</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 1 N = 10<sup>5</sup> dynes</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 1 dyne = 10<sup>5</sup> N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 1 N = 10<sup>7</sup> dynes</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q8.</strong> Action and reaction forces described in Newton's third law of motion:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Act on the same body in opposite directions</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Act on different bodies in opposite directions</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Act on the same body in the same direction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Cancel each other completely</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q9.</strong> A passenger in a moving train throws a coin vertically upward. If the coin falls behind him, the train is moving with:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Uniform velocity</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Acceleration</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Retardation</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Zero acceleration</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q10.</strong> Which of the following is an example of a non-contact force?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Frictional force</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Normal reaction force</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Electrostatic force</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Tension in a string</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q11.</strong> The rate of change of linear momentum of a body is directly proportional to the:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Displacement</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Applied force</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Velocity</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Time taken</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q12.</strong> A body of mass m moving with speed v strikes a wall normally and rebounds with the same speed. The magnitude of change in momentum is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 0</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) mv</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 2mv</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">1</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2</span></span>mv</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q13.</strong> The weight of a body of mass 5 kg at a place where g = 9.8 m s<sup>-2</sup> is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 49 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 5 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 9.8 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 50 N</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q14.</strong> A constant force produces an acceleration of 2 m s<sup>-2</sup> in a body of mass 1 kg. The same force acting on a body of mass 2 kg produces an acceleration of:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 4 m s<sup>-2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 1 m s<sup>-2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 2 m s<sup>-2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 0.5 m s<sup>-2</sup></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q15.</strong> When no external force acts on a body, which of the following quantities remains constant?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Velocity</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Linear momentum</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Acceleration</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Both (a) and (b)</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section B: Fill in the Blanks</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Fill in the blanks with appropriate scientific terms.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> The property of an object to resist any change in its state of rest or uniform motion is called _______.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> According to Newton's second law, net force is equal to the product of mass and _______.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> To every action force, there is always an equal and _______ reaction force.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> The CGS unit of force is _______.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> The gravitational force exerted by the Earth on a body is known as its _______.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section C: True / False</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: State whether the following statements are True or False.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Action and reaction forces act on the same body simultaneously.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Inertia of a body is directly proportional to its mass.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Linear momentum is a scalar quantity because mass is a scalar.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> Non-contact forces can act on a body even when it is not in physical contact with the source of the force.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> An unbalanced force applied to a body can change both its speed and direction of motion.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section D: Very Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Answer the following questions briefly.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> State Newton's First Law of Motion.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Define 1 Newton of force in terms of S.I. units.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Why does a standing passenger fall forward when a fast-moving bus suddenly stops?</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> State two key differences between mass and weight of an object.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> A body of mass 50 kg is accelerated uniformly from a velocity of 4 m s<sup>-1</sup> to 10 m s<sup>-1</sup> in 3 seconds. Calculate the magnitude of the force acting on the body.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section E: Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Answer the following questions in detail.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> State Newton's Second Law of Motion. Mathematical derivation: Starting from statement of law, prove that F = ma.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> (a) State Newton's Third Law of Motion.
(b) Explain, using action and reaction forces, why a swimmer pushes water backward in order to move forward.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> A constant force of 20 N acts on a body of mass 4 kg initially at rest. Calculate:
(a) The acceleration produced in the body.
(b) The velocity attained by the body after 5 s.
(c) The distance covered by the body in 5 s</div>
